{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_001 demands the panel override the checklist on trap cases as the hallmark of 'genuine' gain, but this is self-undermining under a demand for a causal mechanism. If panelists can recognize that a checklist item is misleading by deeper features 'visible to intuition' and can reliably override it, then the primary cause of correct performance on those cases is the panelists' own intuition/exception-recognition — not checklist-mediated processing. SRC_001 offers no mechanism showing the checklist causally contributes to correct trap-case outcomes; it merely assumes that because the checklist is 'a genuine cognitive aid,' it 'enhances the panelists' underlying judgment' to recognize exceptions. That is an assertion of the effect, not a mechanism. Worse, it does not specify what observation within a single evaluation would isolate the checklist's causal role versus the panelists' raw judgment: any non-naive design would need a no-checklist trained-panel control on trap cases to separate panel-judgment from checklist-enhancement, and SRC_001 omits that. It also leaves the key mechanistic boundary undefined: an override that merely uses a second surface heuristic is indistinguishable from one guided by genuine insight, so 'the panel overrides the checklist' is not an observable proxy for a checklist-causal gain — it is compatible with the checklist contributing nothing to that outcome.","target":"e582eda02a66a250f1c719e58ff945bdb3a96da1baaba207cfcc9b0525bc3c4d"}]}